<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Macau (Portuguese)</h1><p class="st-meta">1557 CE – 1999 CE · capital Macau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Europe's first and last foothold in China, a Portuguese trading port for over four centuries that returned to Chinese rule in 1999.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Portuguese merchants, after years of informal trade on the south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> coast, were allowed by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> officials to settle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> peninsula around 1557, paying an annual ground rent. The port grew quickly as the link between Ming China and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau's</a> merchants carried Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> and Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the city became a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions to China and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>; the ruined façade of St Paul's dates from this era. A municipal Senate governed with a Portuguese captain, while China kept authority over the Chinese population. The colony repulsed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> attack in 1622, declined when the Japan trade ended in 1639, and later relied on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> trade and gambling.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joao-maria-ferreira-do-amaral">Ferreira do Amaral</a> expelled Chinese officials in 1849, and an 1887 treaty recognised perpetual Portuguese occupation. After Portugal's 1974 revolution, the 1987 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-portuguese-joint-declaration">Joint Declaration</a> arranged its return, and on 20 December 1999 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/special-administrative-regions-of-china">Special Administrative Region</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/macau-portuguese.jpg" alt="Macau (Portuguese)" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: tkl_darkspider · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portuguese_Macau_Unofficial.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1557 CE – 1999 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Macau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>João Maria Ferreira do Amaral, Vasco Rocha Vieira</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cantonese, Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Portuguese Macau, Macao</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1778&amp;focus=macau-portuguese" aria-label="Macau (Portuguese) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7722 1358 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Macau (Portuguese) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8042 1614L4773 1044M8042 1614L7868 1006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8042 1614h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8042 1614h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1778&amp;focus=macau-portuguese">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
